# 877. Stone Game

## 📝 Problem Statement

Alice and Bob are playing a game with an **even number of piles** of stones arranged in a row.

- Alice starts first.
- On each turn, a player removes the **entire leftmost or rightmost pile**.
- Both players play **optimally**.
- The total number of stones is **odd**, so a tie is impossible.

Return **`true`** if Alice wins; otherwise, return **`false`**.

**LeetCode:** https://leetcode.com/problems/stone-game/

---

## 💡 Approach

This problem has a mathematical observation instead of requiring Dynamic Programming.

Since:

- The number of piles is **even**.
- Alice always moves first.

The piles can be divided into:

- Even-indexed piles
- Odd-indexed piles

Before making her first move, Alice compares the total stones in both groups.

She can always force the game so that she collects **all piles from either the even indices or the odd indices**, depending on which group has the larger total.

Because the total number of stones is **odd**, the sums of the two groups cannot be equal. Therefore, one group always contains strictly more stones.

Hence, Alice can always guarantee a win.

